{
  "id": "gru-cell-forward",
  "title": "Build a Mini GRU Cell (Forward Pass)",
  "description": "Implement one forward step of a gated recurrent unit. The update gate is\n\n$$\nz_t = \\sigma(x_tW_z + h_{t-1}U_z + b_z)\n$$\n\nThe reset gate is\n\n$$\nr_t = \\sigma(x_tW_r + h_{t-1}U_r + b_r)\n$$\n\nThe candidate hidden state is\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{h}_t = \\tanh(x_tW_h + (r_t \\odot h_{t-1})U_h + b_h)\n$$\n\nThe new hidden state is\n\n$$\nh_t = (1-z_t) \\odot h_{t-1} + z_t \\odot \\widetilde{h}_t\n$$\n\nHere, $x_t$ has feature width $D$, $h_{t-1}$ has hidden width $H$, $\\sigma$ is the sigmoid function, and $\\odot$ denotes elementwise multiplication. The params dictionary contains Wz, Wr, and Wh with shape $(D,H)$; Uz, Ur, and Uh with shape $(H,H)$; and bz, br, and bh with shape $(H,)$. Support one sample or a batch and return the new hidden state as a NumPy array with the same shape as h_prev.",
  "seoDescription": "Implement a GRU cell forward pass with reset, update, and candidate gates for one sequence timestep.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: x = [[0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0]], h_prev = [[1.0, -1.0], [2.0, 0.0]], every parameter value = 0\n\nOutput: [[0.5, -0.5], [1.0, 0.0]]",
    "Input: x = [0.5, -1.0, 0.0, 0.25, 0.75], h_prev = [0.0, 0.1, -0.1, 0.2], params use the required shapes shown above\n\nOutput: [-0.1115, 0.0543, -0.2421, 0.0817]"
  ],
  "requirements": "* Accept one-dimensional inputs or a two-dimensional batch\n* Use every supplied weight matrix and bias vector\n* Do not modify x, h_prev, or params\n* Return a new NumPy array with the same shape as h_prev\n* Use NumPy only",
  "theory": "## What Is a Mini GRU Cell (Forward Pass)?\n\nIn scientific array computing with NumPy, vectorized tensor operations apply mathematical transformations across entire arrays without Python loop overhead.\n\n$$\nz_t = \\sigma(x_tW_z + h_{t-1}U_z + b_z)\n$$\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulation & Theoretical Mechanics\n\nSupervised and unsupervised learning algorithms minimize empirical risk over a hypothesis space $\\mathcal{H}$ while regularizing model capacity to balance bias and variance on unseen test distributions.\n\nThe full mathematical structure is governed by the coupled relations:\n\n$$\nr_t = \\sigma(x_tW_r + h_{t-1}U_r + b_r)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{h}_t = \\tanh(x_tW_h + (r_t \\odot h_{t-1})U_h + b_h)\n$$\n\n$$\nh_t = (1-z_t) \\odot h_{t-1} + z_t \\odot \\widetilde{h}_t\n$$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{softmax}(z_i) = \\frac{\\exp(z_i - \\max_k z_k)}{\\sum_{j} \\exp(z_j - \\max_k z_k)}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{scale} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\sigma^2 + \\varepsilon}}\n$$\n\n---\n\n## Key Properties & Conceptual Intuition\n\n- **Inductive Bias & Capacity Control:** Regularization penalties ($L_1$, $L_2$, pruning, or ensemble averaging) constrain effective hypothesis complexity and prevent overfitting to training noise.\n- **Decision Boundary Geometry:** Linear models partition feature space with hyperplanes, distance-based methods induce Voronoi tessellations, and tree ensembles form axis-aligned orthotopes.\n- **Related Topics in This Track:** **Candidate Hidden State**, **Complete GRU Cell**, **Complete GRU Network**.\n\n---\n\n## Numerical Stability & Boundary Behavior\n\n- **Conditioning & Degenerate Regimes:** When denominators, norms, or variances approach zero on constant or collinear inputs, additive regularization ($\\varepsilon > 0$) or limiting analytical values prevent division-by-zero instabilities.\n- **Scale & Precision Invariance:** Formulating updates via centered shifts (such as log-sum-exp shifting for exponentials or Welford-style centering for moments) avoids catastrophic cancellation and floating-point overflow.\n\n---\n\n## Computational Complexity\n\n- **Time Complexity:** $\\mathcal{O}(N \\cdot d)$ (or $\\mathcal{O}(N \\cdot M \\cdot d)$ for matrix-matrix products) to evaluate the linear projections and inner products.\n- **Space Complexity:** $\\mathcal{O}(N \\cdot d)$ to store the projected representations and output array.",
